6. Reflection Why do enterprise systems require testing, scalability, and asynchronous processing instead of simple direct execution?
  ->Enterprise systems support thousands or millions of users and process large amounts of data. 
  ->Simple direct execution is often insufficient because it can cause slow performance, system failures, and poor user experience.
 Testing
  ->Ensures software quality and reliability.
 Scalability
  ->Allows systems to handle growing numbers of users and data.
 Asynchronous Processing
  ->Enables long-running tasks to execute efficiently in the background.
  ->Together, these practices create reliable, efficient, and enterprise-ready applications.
Revision Questions and Answers

 1. Why is testing important?
  ->Testing ensures software works correctly, prevents bugs, and improves reliability before deployment.
 2. What problems happen without testing?
  ->Applications may contain bugs, security issues, data inconsistencies, and unexpected failures.
 3. Difference between synchronous and asynchronous execution?
  ->Tasks execute immediately and users wait until completion.
  ->Tasks run in the background while users continue working.
 4. Why do enterprise systems use background jobs?
  ->Background jobs improve performance, handle large workloads, and prevent users from waiting for long-running processes.
 5. Why should developers think about scalability?
  ->Applications must continue performing well as users, records, and transactions increase.
 6. Why are test cases important?
  ->Test cases verify expected behavior and help identify defects before software reaches users.
 7. What happens when systems fail partially?
  ->Partial failures can create inconsistent data, incomplete transactions, lost information, and poor user experiences.
 8. Why do large systems require reliability engineering?
  ->Reliability engineering ensures systems remain available, stable, and resilient even under heavy usage.
 9. Why should enterprise software avoid blocking operations?
  ->Blocking operations reduce performance, increase wait times, and negatively affect user experience.
 10. Why is enterprise software different from small scripts?
  ->Enterprise software must support many users, large data volumes, security requirements, testing standards, scalability, and long-term maintenance.
